If your setup only accepts 127.0.0.1 (likely a typo for 227.0.0.1) and your machine's IP while rejecting 10.0.2.2, your emulator is using a bridged network (common in BlueStacks, Nox, MEmu, or custom VirtualBox setups) rather than standard Android Studio NAT networking.
Because the virtual phone acts like a separate device directly on your Wi-Fi/AP network, it does not recognize the 10.0.2.2 alias.

Method 1: Use adb reverse to Force 127.0.0.1 (Best & Permanent)

Instead of forcing the emulator to accept 10.0.2.2, you can create a direct USB/ADB tunnel so the emulator sends traffic to its own 127.0.0.1, which automatically forwards straight to Burp Suite on your computer. This completely bypasses router and AP IP changes.
1. Bind Burp Suite to Loopback. In Burp Suite, go to Proxy > Proxy Listeners, edit your listener, and bind it to 127.0.0.1 (or All interfaces) on port 8080.
2. Create the ADB Tunnel. Open your computer's terminal or command prompt and run:bash
adb reverse tcp:8080 tcp:8080
Use code with caution.
3. Set Emulator Proxy to 127.0.0.1. Inside your virtual phone's Wi-Fi settings, set the Proxy hostname to 127.0.0.1 and Proxy port to 8080.
4. Verify the Connection. Your emulator will now route all traffic through the ADB cable/bridge directly into Burp Suite, regardless of whether AP mode is on or your router changes your IP. (Note: You only need to re-run the adb reverse command if you completely restart the emulator).

Method 2: Check Why 10.0.2.2 Was Rejected (If Using Android Studio)

If you are using the official Android Studio Emulator and 10.0.2.2 fails, two settings usually block it:
• Burp Suite Listener Scope. In Burp Suite under Proxy > Proxy Listeners, if your listener is strictly bound to your Wi-Fi IP (192.168.x.x), it will reject 10.0.2.2 because 10.0.2.2 arrives via 127.0.0.1. Change the Burp listener binding to All interfaces or 127.0.0.1.
• No Spaces in the IP. Ensure there are no accidental spaces when typing 10.0.2.2 into the Android Wi-Fi proxy box, as Android's Save button will gray out or reject the input.

Method 3: Add a Virtual Loopback Adapter (If ADB Reverse Fails)

If your emulator blocks ADB reverse tunneling, you can give your computer a permanent secondary IP address that never changes.
• Windows. Open Device Manager, add a legacy Microsoft KM-TEST Loopback Adapter, and assign it a static IP like 10.10.10.10.
• macOS / Linux. Run sudo ifconfig lo0 alias 10.10.10.10 (Mac) or sudo ip addr add 10.10.10.10/24 dev lo (Linux).
• Connect. Bind Burp Suite to All interfaces and point your emulator's proxy to 10.10.10.10.
